"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,482\nHabits, not willpower\n\n4\n00:00:12,282 --> 00:00:16,236\nYou might think the students who get the most done simply have more willpower.\n\n5\n00:00:16,659 --> 00:00:18,563\nThe research points somewhere else.\n\n6\n00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:20,656\nThey lean less on willpower,\n\n7\n00:00:20,676 --> 00:00:22,061\nand more on habits.\n\n8\n00:00:22,231 --> 00:00:25,018\nA habit is a behaviour set off by a cue,\n\n9\n00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:27,002\nlike a time or a place,\n\n10\n00:00:27,256 --> 00:00:29,089\nrather than by a fresh decision.\n\n11\n00:00:29,631 --> 00:00:32,519\nIn one study at University College London,\n\n12\n00:00:32,737 --> 00:00:37,771\nninety six volunteers repeated a single daily behaviour after a fixed cue,\n\n13\n00:00:37,916 --> 00:00:39,330\nsuch as after breakfast,\n\n14\n00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:40,792\nfor twelve weeks.\n\n15\n00:00:40,962 --> 00:00:43,156\nFor those whose data fit the model,\n\n16\n00:00:43,422 --> 00:00:48,076\nthe median time to reach near full automaticity was sixty six days.\n\n17\n00:00:48,491 --> 00:00:53,759\nBut the range ran from eighteen days to an estimated two hundred and fifty four.\n\n18\n00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,822\nAnd missing a single day did not derail the process.\n\n19\n00:00:57,992 --> 00:01:01,010\nSo why does self control predict good outcomes?\n\n20\n00:01:01,399 --> 00:01:11,362\nAcross six studies, researchers at the University of Pennsylvania found that people with better self control reported resisting temptation less often,\n\n21\n00:01:11,686 --> 00:01:12,437\nnot more.\n\n22\n00:01:13,066 --> 00:01:17,820\nTheir study habits explained much of the link to grades and staying in college.\n\n23\n00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:21,442\nYou may have heard that willpower is a tank that runs dry.\n\n24\n00:01:21,959 --> 00:01:24,778\nEarlier studies suggested a sizable effect.\n\n25\n00:01:25,174 --> 00:01:31,764\nBut a replication across twenty three labs and more than two thousand people found an effect close to zero.\n\n26\n00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:33,575\nThe debate goes on,\n\n27\n00:01:33,823 --> 00:01:37,640\nso planning your day around a fuel tank is a shaky strategy.\n\n28\n00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:38,789\nSome caution.\n\n29\n00:01:39,208 --> 00:01:40,846\nThe habit study was small,\n\n30\n00:01:40,961 --> 00:01:44,505\nand about half the volunteers never showed a clear habit curve.\n\n31\n00:01:45,086 --> 00:01:47,959\nThe self control findings are correlations.\n\n32\n00:01:48,202 --> 00:01:51,926\nAnd more complex behaviours took longer to become automatic.\n\n33\n00:01:52,096 --> 00:01:52,947\nTry this.\n\n34\n00:01:53,253 --> 00:01:55,654\nPick one fixed time and place for study.\n\n35\n00:01:55,945 --> 00:01:58,037\nAttach it to something you already do,\n\n36\n00:01:58,057 --> 00:01:59,148\nlike after lunch.\n\n37\n00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:02,179\nStart small enough to manage on a bad day.\n\n38\n00:02:02,468 --> 00:02:03,696\nAnd if you miss a day,\n\n39\n00:02:03,716 --> 00:02:05,365\nsimply start again tomorrow.\n\n40\n00:02:05,535 --> 00:02:07,225\nWillpower gets you started.\n\n41\n00:02:07,431 --> 00:02:08,866\nHabits keep you going.\n\n"
